#let terms = (
  (slug: "term-client", title: [Client], body: [An immutable handle binding one validated role configuration to restart-stable process names. A correlated client view shares those processes.]),
  (slug: "term-provider", title: [Provider], body: [The configured OpenID issuer and the endpoints and signing keys authenticated through its discovery and transport policy.]),
  (slug: "term-cache-incarnation", title: [Cache incarnation], body: [One live provider-cache process with its own accepted snapshot and fetch ownership. A replacement process has a different incarnation even when its registered name is unchanged.]),
  (slug: "term-provider-snapshot", title: [Provider snapshot], body: [The metadata and signing-key set accepted by one cache incarnation. It is observed provider evidence, not a substitute for configured policy.]),
  (slug: "term-login-transaction", title: [Login transaction], body: [A pending authorization-code login bound to one client's issuer and client id, exact redirect, state, nonce, PKCE verifier and expiry. It is sealed into a cookie that only the starting browser holds; the server keeps no copy.]),
  (slug: "term-login-cookie", title: [Login cookie], body: [The browser cookie that holds one sealed login transaction, named by a digest of its state and scoped to the callback path. It is distinct from the application's authenticated session.]),
  (slug: "term-verified-identity", title: [Verified identity], body: [The checked identity evidence of a session: the login claims and the current claims under one stable key, the pair of issuer and subject.]),
  (slug: "term-login-claims", title: [Login claims], body: [The claims of the ID token verified for the consumed login transaction. They are stored apart, never replaced, and are the only source of authentication time, ACR and AMR.]),
  (slug: "term-current-claims", title: [Current claims], body: [The claims of the latest verified ID token held in custody. They equal the login claims until a refresh returns an ID token, which replaces them whole.]),
  (slug: "term-claims-age", title: [Claims age], body: [Now minus the issue time of the current claims. A caller bounds it on a checked read. It is unrelated to the OIDC `max_age` request parameter and says nothing about when the provider last evaluated the claims.]),
  (slug: "term-renewal-circuit", title: [Renewal circuit], body: [A pause on refreshes started only to renew claims, held by one client's provider cache on one node, so a client with a separate provider cache is not paused and a cache restart clears it, opened when one such refresh ends with the provider's outcome unknown, with a rate-limit answer, or without claims that satisfy the bound. After the window one refresh is the probe and the rest stay paused until it reports. A response rejected for one session does not open it. It protects other sessions from the same provider fault.]),
  (slug: "term-session", title: [Session], body: [An opaque view of confirmed custody containing verified identity, reference and revision. It is neither a token bag nor a business permission grant.]),
  (slug: "term-session-reference", title: [Session reference], body: [A random bearer value naming custody. A reference proves neither that custody still exists nor that the caller may perform a business operation.]),
  (slug: "term-custody", title: [Custody], body: [Hellhound's authority over session identity, authentication evidence and token generations in sealed records. It owns installation, refresh transitions and termination.]),
  (slug: "term-write-condition", title: [Write condition], body: [The atomic admission requirement for a record write: key absence, an expected store version, or that version before a separate validity deadline.]),
  (slug: "term-store-version", title: [Store version], body: [The compare-and-set concurrency value of a stored row. It is distinct from the token revision and advances on record writes such as refresh reservation or idle touch.]),
  (slug: "term-token-revision", title: [Token revision], body: [The generation number of the material exposed by a session. It advances when refreshed material is published.]),
  (slug: "term-refresh-dispatch", title: [Refresh dispatch], body: [The fresh custody reservation authorizing one provider refresh call for one token revision. Possession of an old session or a waiting result does not create it.]),
  (slug: "term-refresh-lease", title: [Refresh lease], body: [The wall-clock interval during which an outstanding refresh reservation may settle normally. Expiry withdraws permission to resend the predecessor.]),
  (slug: "term-quarantine", title: [Quarantine], body: [Custody's refusal to dispatch a token generation whose provider outcome or accepted response is unsafe to reuse. It retains uncertainty rather than proving revocation.]),
  (slug: "term-custody-recovery", title: [Custody recovery], body: [An owner-bound opaque installation command retained after uncertain custody acknowledgement. It can repeat installation of the same evidence without repeating code exchange.]),
  (slug: "term-refresh-recovery", title: [Refresh recovery], body: [An owner-bound opaque publication command holding already received refreshed material. It cannot authorize another provider request.]),
  (slug: "term-send-evidence", title: [Send evidence], body: [The distinction between proven no transmission and possible provider receipt. A timeout or connection failure after submission cannot prove no send.]),
  (slug: "term-access-claims", title: [Access claims], body: [An opaque checked JWT access-token view for one validator's issuer and resource policy. Application authorization remains a separate decision.]),
  (slug: "term-action", title: [Action], body: [The closed caller response classification Reauthenticate, RetryLater, Recover, FixConfiguration or RejectRequest. It classifies recovery meaning rather than generic retryability.]),
  (slug: "term-sealing-key-ring", title: [Sealing key ring], body: [One current AES-256-GCM key and retired keys permitted for opening older records. Its lifecycle is controlled by the application for durable stores.]),
  (slug: "term-write-outcome", title: [Write outcome], body: [The confirmed result of a conditional record write: Written, VersionConflict or DeadlineElapsed. An unconfirmed result is a store error, never a write outcome.]),
  (slug: "term-validity-deadline", title: [Validity deadline], body: [The wall-clock instant before which a guarded write may replace a record. It is independent of the record's retention expiry and of the acknowledgement time.]),
  (slug: "term-relying-party", title: [Relying party], body: [The role that signs browser users in through authorization-code login and holds their token custody.]),
  (slug: "term-service-client", title: [Service client], body: [The confidential role that obtains its own access tokens without a login redirect or a user session.]),
  (slug: "term-resource-server", title: [Resource server], body: [The role that validates access tokens against one configured audience and holds no client identity, grants or introspection.]),
  (slug: "term-public-origin", title: [Public origin], body: [The scheme, host and port at which browsers reach the application's login, callback and logout routes. Registered callback addresses are built from it.]),
  (slug: "term-oracle", title: [Oracle], body: [A pinned independent implementation, token corpus or conformance suite whose behavior bounds a named Hellhound contract. It is never a runtime dependency and confers no certification.]),
  (slug: "term-evidence-receipt", title: [Evidence receipt], body: [A retained raw output of one check run with its exact revisions. It bounds the claim it supports and neither certifies a release nor qualifies a later dependency resolution.]),
  (slug: "term-tombstone", title: [Tombstone], body: [A bounded terminal record preserving termination evidence after secret material is removed. It prevents late installation recovery within its retention horizon.]),
  (slug: "term-signed-assertion", title: [Signed assertion], body: [An opaque signed JWT credential containing caller-configured claims. Its signature does not make its claims Hellhound-verified identity or access claims.]),
  (slug: "term-exchange-client", title: [Exchange client], body: [An immutable handle binding explicit transport policy to an application-supervised HTTP pool, independently of OpenID discovery and user custody.]),
  (slug: "term-exchange-token", title: [Exchange token], body: [An opaque provider credential admitted by a caller-owned response decoder, with optional observed expiry. It carries no checked identity or business permission authority.]),
)
